<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.SearchRow"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        WHAT THE WINDOW IS ASKED - the one field, and the count that answers it. It stood in
        MainWindow.xaml until the scope switch arrived, and it moved for the same reason FilterRow
        moved before it: that file was standing exactly on the markup ceiling, so the next thing to
        go in needed something else to come out first.

        THE SEAM IS A SUBJECT RATHER THAN A LINE COUNT, which is the rule FilterRow set. This is the
        QUESTION and the ANSWER TO IT - a field somebody types into and the line saying how many
        entries came back. The scope switch above it is a different subject: it decides WHICH LIST
        is being asked, not what is being asked of it.

        THE COUNT COMES WITH THE FIELD RATHER THAN STAYING BEHIND, and that is deliberate. Complaint
        8 of the eleven moved this line up here to sit beside the box that changes it, because
        beside the box is where somebody is already looking. Splitting them now would undo that with
        a file boundary.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl IS a ContentControl and a
        ContentControl is BOTH BY DEFAULT - measured, not assumed. Without them this row is a tab
        stop that does nothing, standing in front of the field it holds. `docs/10` trap 8.

        One field for all three of A1, A2 and A3, which is the point rather than a saving: a person
        types a word, learns one operator, and never moves to a different box. The same text handed
        to the query switch of bws list picks the same entries.
    -->
    <Grid>
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="{StaticResource WidthCountLine}" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!--
            THE SYNTAX LEFT THE PLACEHOLDER ON 2026-08-12 AND THE BUTTON THAT TOOK IT LEFT ON
            2026-08-13 - owner's decision. It was called "Examples" and it was one click away
            from the box those examples go into, which is one click too many for the thing this
            window is best at. `docs/11` section 6: the language has to be discoverable from the
            field it is typed into.

            It is all in the tooltip below now, composed in QueryExamples.cs - which also argues
            why a composed string rather than a panel of controls.
        -->
        <Grid Grid.Column="0" Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
            <!--
                Updated on every keystroke rather than when the field loses focus. The
                filtering is the feature, and a list that waits for Tab is a list that
                does not filter as you type.

                THE TOOLTIP IS THE ONLY PLACE THE QUERY LANGUAGE IS EXPLAINED, so it carries
                what can be typed, the one piece of syntax nobody guesses - a regular expression
                between slashes - and the questions to start from that fit the list on screen
                (backlog 358: three of the six selected nothing on Drivers). Bound on the BOX rather than
                inside the tooltip: a tooltip hangs off a Popup, which is not in the visual tree,
                and what a Popup inherits is a question this window has already paid to learn.
            -->
            <!--
                A FIELD THAT CAN SAY IT IS WRONG SINCE 2026-09-23 - UX-GUI-002. While the query has
                a mistake in it the box wears the red edge the box of seconds on the plan sheet
                wears, through the same style: the model answers INotifyDataErrorInfo for
                QueryText, the binding hears it by itself. ReportingBox in Controls.xaml carries
                why each part of it is shaped the way it is.
            -->
            <TextBox x:Name="QueryBox"
                     Style="{StaticResource ReportingBox}"
                     ToolTip="{Binding SearchTip}"
                     Text="{Binding QueryText, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged, Delay=400}" />

            <!--
                The hint, behind the field and out of the way of the mouse. An empty box
                teaches nobody that the language exists, and this is the one place it can
                be shown without spending a line of the window on it.

                BOUND TO THE MODEL RATHER THAN A FIXED KEY SINCE 2026-09-15, point 8(b) of
                `docs/11` 2.14: it named services AND drivers on a list from which, by its own
                tooltip, a search never reaches a driver. MainViewModel.SearchHint says what the
                list somebody is standing on holds.
            -->
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding SearchHint}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource PadCell}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       IsHitTestVisible="False">
                <TextBlock.Style>
                    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource SubduedText}">
                        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                        <Style.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Text, ElementName=QueryBox}" Value="">
                                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </Style.Triggers>
                    </Style>
                </TextBlock.Style>
            </TextBlock>

            <!--
                THE LIST UNDER THE FIELD - point 9 of `docs/11` 2.14, backlog 15. What can be
                written where the caret stands, offered as somebody types, and the questions to
                start from - the ones the list on screen can answer - when somebody arrives at an
                empty box. The language was explained in
                the tooltip alone until today, and a person who does not know what goes in a box
                CLICKS it rather than pointing.

                A Popup of our own rather than the library's AutoSuggestBox, and the argument is
                measured rather than preferred: that control filters by the WHOLE text, replaces
                the whole text on a choice, wraps a TextBox of its own type our style cannot reach,
                and handles Escape inside a control this window already takes Escape from first.
                `docs/PROJEKT-PODPOWIEDZI-20260915.md` section 3.

                StaysOpen, and the closing is the window's - on the box losing the keyboard, on
                Escape, on a choice, on the window moving or losing activation, and when there is
                nothing left to offer. A popup that closes itself on an outside click takes that
                click with it, and the click somebody most often makes while the list is open is
                on the box it hangs off.

                Nothing in here is focusable. The keyboard stays in the box while the list is
                open, which is what makes typing through the list possible - `docs/10` trap 8 is
                why every part says so in its own style rather than leaving it to the popup.

                Opaque, with square corners. A transparent popup is a layered window and a
                layered window loses ClearType - `docs/10` trap 3 - so the rounded corner the
                library's menus wear would cost the sharpness of every word in the list. Backlog
                359 carries the question.
            -->
            <Popup x:Name="SuggestionPopup"
                   PlacementTarget="{Binding ElementName=QueryBox}"
                   Placement="Bottom"
                   IsOpen="{Binding Suggesting.IsOpen, Mode=OneWay}"
                   StaysOpen="True"
                   AllowsTransparency="False"
                   Focusable="False">
                <Border Style="{StaticResource SuggestionPanel}"
                        Width="{Binding ActualWidth, ElementName=QueryBox}">
                    <DockPanel>
                        <!--
                            WHAT THIS LIST IS, SAID OVER IT, 2026-09-16: "Questions to start from"
                            over the examples on an empty box, "What can go here" over completions.
                            Five rows of syntax with no sentence about what they were was half of
                            the owner's "unintuitive" - the caption is that sentence.
                        -->
                        <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top"
                                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="suggestCaption"
                                   Text="{Binding Suggesting.Caption}"
                                   Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}"
                                   Margin="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
                        <!--
                            THE KEYS, WRITTEN WHERE THEY ARE USED. A sentence in the tooltip would
                            repeat the mistake point 9 corrects - explaining a thing in a place
                            nobody looks. Under the list is the one place somebody is looking
                            while the keys matter. In a foot of their own since 2026-09-16, with
                            the rule the plan sheet draws over its footer, so they do not read as
                            one more row.
                        -->
                        <Border DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Style="{StaticResource SuggestionFoot}">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Suggesting.Keys}"
                                       Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />
                        </Border>
                        <ListBox x:Name="SuggestionList"
                                 AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Suggestions"
                                 Style="{StaticResource SuggestionList}"
                                 ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource SuggestionItem}"
                                 ItemTemplate="{StaticResource SuggestionRow}"
                                 ItemsSource="{Binding Suggesting.Offered}"
                                 SelectedItem="{Binding Suggesting.Chosen, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                 Grid.IsSharedSizeScope="True"
                                 VirtualizingPanel.IsVirtualizing="False" />
                    </DockPanel>
                </Border>
            </Popup>
        </Grid>

        <!--
            THE DRIVERS CHECKBOX STOOD HERE UNTIL 2026-08-11 AND IS NOW A CHIP IN THE ROW
            BELOW. It was A5's promise arriving early - one control that read the query and
            wrote into it - and A5 arrived, so it joined the others rather than sitting
            beside them meaning the same thing in a different shape. docs/11 complaint 7
            left the grouping of this row deliberately unfinished for exactly this moment.
        -->

        <!--
            THE COUNT, AND IT MOVED UP HERE FROM UNDER THE LIST. Complaint 8 of the eleven:
            it was the least visible thing on the screen - small, grey, bottom left - and it
            is the main answer to every keystroke in the box beside it. Beside the box is
            where somebody is already looking.

            It doubles as complaint 7, "you cannot see THAT it is filtering". A narrowed list
            reads "108 of 810 entries" rather than "810 entries", which says both that a
            filter is on and how much it took - and the style below makes it semibold when
            narrowed, so the difference is visible without reading the number.

            Trimmed with a tooltip, because this line also carries the failure message, which
            is the system's and can be any length. The same failure is said again in the
            middle of the empty list, where there is room for it.
        -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="countLine"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   Text="{Binding Says.Status}"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   ToolTip="{Binding Says.Status}"
                   Style="{StaticResource CountLine}" />

        <!--
            WHAT THE ANSWER TO THIS QUESTION HAS TO SAY ABOUT ITSELF, UNDER THE BOX THAT ASKED IT -
            UX-GUI-002 and 009 of the audit of 2026-09-23. A mistake in the query, in red, with the
            sentence that the list below is the previous answer - or else, in the notice colour,
            what the answer could not judge or has not read yet. Until that day both stood at the
            foot of the window, about 830 pixels below the box, in a line shared with the session's
            rights and the folded instances.

            UNDER THE BOX AND NOT UNDER THE COUNT - it is a sentence about the question, and the
            count beside it has its own width to keep.

            IT TAKES ROOM WHILE THERE IS TEXT IN THE BOX, whether or not it has words - Says
            decides that, because a view that counts is logic (GUI rule 11). The chrome already
            takes nearly half the window's height (UX-GUI-007), so the line is not always there -
            and a line tied to its words would move the list each time a reading note came and
            went under somebody's typing. Tied to the box, it moves with a key somebody pressed.

            The list under the box, when it is open, lies over this line - the red edge on the box
            stays in sight. Moving that popup below the line is recorded as an observation, not
            done here.
        -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1"
                   Grid.Column="0"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="answerLine"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginUnderField}"
                   Text="{Binding Says.AnswerLine}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap">
            <TextBlock.Style>
                <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource NoticeText}">
                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                    <Style.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.AnswerLineShown}" Value="True">
                            <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.AnswerLineIsProblem}" Value="True">
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </Style.Triggers>
                </Style>
            </TextBlock.Style>
        </TextBlock>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
